{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{# DECISIONS 0030. The only Amazon links are the macro's: search links, tagged,
   rel-marked, each with the labels the GA4 click handler reads. #}
{% macro links(key) %}
<ul>
  {% for g in guide[key] %}
  <li><a href="{{ g.url }}" rel="{{ affiliate_rel }}" data-ga-event="gear_click" data-ga-placement="gear_guide" data-ga-type="amazon_search">{{ g.label }}</a></li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endmacro %}
{% macro disclosure(anchor_id) %}
<p class="muted" id="{{ anchor_id }}">
  The gear links on this page are affiliate links: if you buy something after
  following one, this site earns a commission, and it costs you nothing extra.
  {{ amazon_disclosure }}
  {{ gear_endorsement }}
  <a href="{{ root }}privacy/#affiliate">More in the privacy policy</a>.
</p>
{% endmacro %}
{% block content %}
<h1>What to bring fishing at a California trout water</h1>
<p class="lede">
  A short packing list for a day at a California lake, pond or stream that is
  on the planting schedule. It is general fishing know-how, not a product
  review. Bring what the day and the water call for, and leave the rest.
</p>
{{ disclosure("gear-disclosure") }}
<p>
  Each list below ends with links that search Amazon.com for that kind of
  item. They point to search results, not to any one product, and this page
  names no brand and compares no prices.
</p>

<h2 id="license">License and rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Anglers 16 and older generally need a California fishing license. The
      Department of Fish and Wildlife explains the types and how to get one on
      its <a href="{{ cdfw_license_url }}">fishing license page</a>. Check
      there, because rules and fees change.</li>
  <li>Rules differ by water. Some waters limit the bait, the hooks or the
      number of fish you may keep, and parks and reservoirs may charge a
      day-use fee or post rules of their own. Read the signs at the water.</li>
  <li>A water&rsquo;s page here shows what CDFW has scheduled, by week, never
      by day. Plants are subject to change, so a scheduled week is not a
      promise that fish are in the water on the day you arrive.</li>
</ul>
<p class="muted">
  This is a reminder, not legal advice. CDFW&rsquo;s own pages are the
  authority on licenses and regulations.
</p>

<h2 id="species">Trout and catfish: what differs</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Trout</strong> prefer cooler, well-oxygenated water. They are
      often more active in the cool hours near sunrise and sunset. Fish at a planting water are commonly rainbow trout, and many anglers
      fish for them with light line, small hooks, dough baits, worms or small
      spinners and spoons.</li>
  <li><strong>Catfish</strong> favor warmer water and often feed near the
      bottom, with evening and night often the more productive hours in
      summer. They run bigger and pull harder, so many anglers step up to a
      stouter rod and heavier line than they would for trout, and use cut
      bait, chicken liver, worms or prepared catfish baits. Some waters on
      the schedule list catfish; check the water&rsquo;s page for the species
      CDFW shows.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tackle">Rod, reel, line and small tackle</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A light spinning rod around six to seven feet, with a matching reel, is
      a common choice for trout at a lake or pond.</li>
  <li>Line in the four to six pound range is common for trout. Clear
      monofilament or fluorocarbon is less visible in the water.</li>
  <li>Small hooks, split shot, a few bobbers and a swivel or two cover most
      bait setups. Pinching a barb flat is required at some waters and kinder
      to the fish everywhere.</li>
  <li>Needle-nose pliers and a line cutter make removing a hook much easier.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("rod") }}

<h2 id="bait">Bait and lures for trout</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Dough baits, worms and salmon eggs are common bait choices where bait is
      allowed. Some waters allow artificial lures, not bait, so check the rules
      before you buy bait.</li>
  <li>Small spinners and spoons are common lures for casting from shore, and
      they are easy to carry in a pocket.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("bait") }}

<h2 id="catfish">If you are after catfish</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A medium-power rod with ten to twenty pound line is a common starting
      point, with a sliding sinker rig that keeps the bait on the bottom.</li>
  <li>Circle hooks usually hook the fish in the corner of the mouth, which
      makes release easier.</li>
  <li>A headlamp or lantern helps if you stay after dark, and a folding chair
      helps while you wait.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("catfish") }}

<h2 id="cold">Cold, wet and shoulder-season days</h2>
<p>
  Mornings at a mountain lake or a foothill stream can be cold even when the
  afternoon warms up. The weather in late fall, winter and spring can change
  within hours.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Dress in layers you can remove: a base layer, an insulating layer and a
      shell that blocks wind and rain.</li>
  <li>A warm hat and gloves matter more than they seem. Gloves with free
      fingertips, or ones you can slip off, help with knots.</li>
  <li>Waterproof boots keep feet dry on a muddy bank. Waders are for wading;
      stay out of fast or deep water, and wear a life jacket on a boat.</li>
  <li>A thermos of something hot is welcome after an hour of standing still.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("cold") }}

<h2 id="sun">Sun and summer days</h2>
<p>
  Shade can be scarce on a lake shore, and the sun reflects off the water.
  Summer days can be hot, and fish are often more active early in the morning
  or in the evening, so many people arrive early and leave before midday.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>A wide-brim hat, sunscreen and sunglasses protect you for hours outside.
      Polarized lenses also cut glare, which makes it easier to see into the
      water.</li>
  <li>Bring more drinking water than you expect to need, and snacks.</li>
  <li>Insect repellent is useful near still water at dusk.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("sun") }}

<h2 id="cooler">Cooler and cleaning</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Know the daily limit and any size rule for the water before you keep a
      fish. If you plan to release fish, wet your hands before you touch
      the fish and handle it as little as you can.</li>
  <li>Fish keep better cold. A cooler with ice, brought from home, lets you
      chill a catch right away; a stringer or a bucket works for a short wait.</li>
  <li>A sharp fillet knife, a small cutting board, zip bags and wet wipes
      make cleaning simpler. Follow any posted rules about cleaning fish at
      the water.</li>
  <li>A landing net helps bring a fish in without losing it.</li>
  <li>Pack out your trash, including loose line and hooks.</li>
</ul>
{{ links("cooler") }}

<h2 id="before">Before you go</h2>
<p>
  <a href="{{ root }}">See what is scheduled this week</a>, or find a water
  <a href="{{ root }}county/">by county</a>. Each water&rsquo;s page shows its
  scheduled weeks, and CDFW&rsquo;s own
  <a href="{{ attribution_url }}">Fish Planting Schedule</a> is the source.
</p>
{{ disclosure("gear-disclosure-end") }}
{% endblock %}
